# =================================================================
# CYBERDUDEBIVASH® SENTINEL APEX v101 — MASTER IGNORE FILE
# Last enforced: v101.0.0 — PERMANENT PRODUCTION CONTRACT
# Architecture: Runtime-generated volatile data excluded;
#               Pipeline-critical state files EXPLICITLY ALLOWED.
# =================================================================

# --- SOVEREIGNTY & AUTH (NEVER PUSH) ---
secrets/
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
credentials/
token.json
credentials.json
.env
# SEC-2026-07-18: api/auth.py's APIKeyManager bootstraps a live plaintext
# MSSP admin key + key registry here on first run. Runtime-generated
# secret material — must never be tracked. See incident fix commit for
# details on the leaked key this closes.
data/auth/
# SEC-2026-07-25: same class of issue as data/auth/ above. tenants.json held
# real plaintext customer keys, force-committed on every purchase by
# revenue-orchestrator.yml (now fixed to sync via Cloudflare KV instead —
# see that workflow's SEC-2026-07-25 comment). marketplace_state.json holds
# a similar plaintext-key pattern with no active re-commit workflow found;
# flagged for the same rotation/removal treatment. Gitignored so neither can
# be re-added without an explicit `git add -f` override.
data/sovereign/tenants.json
data/marketplace/marketplace_state.json

# --- CUSTOMER PII / PAYMENT RECORDS (this repo is PUBLIC — never commit real
# customer data) ---
# scripts/customer_onboard.py (the operator tool used to provision real paying
# customers: name, email, company, country, payment reference) persists to
# these exact paths, and none of them were previously gitignored — meaning the
# very first real `provision` run followed by a normal `git add`/commit would
# have pushed live customer PII and payment references to a public GitHub
# repository. Verified via full git history: both files have only ever
# contained their zero-record schema skeleton (single commit, b95242ca),
# i.e. this tool has never actually been used yet — so gitignoring now, before
# first use, involves no data loss. data/customers/schema.json and
# data/subscriptions/schema.json are pure JSON Schema (no PII) and stay
# tracked as API documentation. data/welcome_packages/ is the same script's
# per-customer welcome-package output directory (contains the issued API key)
# and is gitignored pre-emptively since it does not exist yet either.
data/customers/registry.json
data/subscriptions/active.json
data/payment_audit.jsonl
data/welcome_packages/

# --- ENTERPRISE SELLABLE ASSETS (protect revenue, upload via Gumroad engine) ---
data/rule_packs/
data/playbooks/
data/certificates/
data/enterprise_kits/
data/temp/

# --- RUNTIME-VOLATILE DATA (generated fresh each cycle — NOT committed) ---
data/blogger_processed.json
# scripts/intelligence_integrity_gate.py's Gate D fetches this live from
# CISA on every run and writes it here as a same-run cache (see that
# script's "[D] KEV catalog fetched live from CISA" log line) -- no history
# for this path exists anywhere in this repo. Committing a snapshot would
# go stale (CISA updates the catalog continuously) and create needless diff
# noise on every local/CI run.
data/kev/
data/sentinel_apex.db
data/manifest_audit.json
data/manifest_backups/
data/.manifest_backups/
data/manifest_versions/
data/logs/
*.log

# Phase 8 storage-sanitation backups (scripts/storage_governance.py):
# verified copies of files kept as a same-session/same-disk restore safety
# net before a delete is allowed to proceed. Committing these would defeat
# the purpose of the cleanup they back up. The durable rollback for anything
# that was git-tracked is `git revert` against history, not this directory --
# see the deletion_manifest_*.json / rollback_manifest_*.json in
# data/governance/, which ARE committed as the audit trail.
data/governance/deletion_backups/

# --- HIGH-VOLUME STIX INDIVIDUAL OBJECTS ---
# These are large individual advisory JSON files — excluded from tracking.
data/stix/CDB-APEX-*.json

# --- SECURITY v109: PRIVATE INTEL DATA — NEVER COMMIT TO PUBLIC REPO ---
# These files flow to Cloudflare R2 via r2-data-sync.yml workflow ONLY.
# Committed to GitHub = auth bypass. Do not un-ignore these lines.
data/stix/feed_manifest.json
data/feed_manifest.json
api/feed.json
api/apex_v2/

# P0 FIX: data/processed_intel.json (SHA256 dedup fingerprint store) belongs
# in this block alongside feed_manifest.json above -- same file family, same
# migration (see scripts/r2_state_sync.py), R2-authoritative for the same
# reason: both multi-source-intel.yml's and sentinel-blogger.yml's direct
# `git push origin main` for it were being rejected on every run since main
# started requiring PRs (~2026-08-26), silently discarding it every time.
data/processed_intel.json

# CodeRabbit review finding on the migration above (verified, not taken on
# faith): multi-source-intel.yml kept `git add -f`-ing these 5 registry
# files + the advisories/ chunk directory after the block above was pulled
# off that same push -- but main's branch ruleset rejects ANY direct push
# to main, not just pushes of specific files, so that push was always going
# to keep failing regardless of which files remained on it, and PR #330
# made that failure hard-fail the job. Same root cause, same fix, same file
# family as the block above -- see scripts/r2_state_sync.py's STATE_FILES /
# STATE_DIRS for the R2 side of this migration.
data/intelligence_repository/intelligence_index.json
data/intelligence_repository/advisory_registry.json
data/intelligence_repository/intel_retention_registry.json
data/intelligence_repository/intel_lifecycle_registry.json
data/intelligence_repository/historical_feed_registry.json
data/intelligence_repository/advisories/

# scripts/r2_lifecycle_manager.py (a manual admin tool, not a scheduled
# pipeline capability -- see that script's own LIFECYCLE_REPORT_PATH
# comment) writes this locally as an observable record of the last
# --verify/--apply outcome. Never committed to main (confirmed: no prior
# entry in git history) -- surfaced as an untracked-file nuisance the
# first time this repo's test suite (tests/test_r2_lifecycle_manager.py)
# happened to exercise the script locally. Same category as the
# rx_pub_a0 artifact manifest immediately below: a local diagnostic
# snapshot, not a distribution artifact.
data/quality/r2_lifecycle_report.json

# scripts/r2_reports_verifier.py (STAGE 3.6a) writes this locally as an
# intermediate step, then syncs it to R2 (BUCKET_DATA, intel/ prefix) itself
# -- per the script's own comments, that R2 sync is the actual distribution
# path (workers/intel-gateway/src/rx-pub-a0-handlers.js reads it from there
# via the /api/v1/rx-pub-a0/reports-identity endpoint), not a git commit.
# Never tracked on main; confirmed via `git ls-tree -r origin/main` before
# adding this line. Any local write (e.g. from running tests or the script
# directly in a sandbox with no real R2 credentials) has pipeline_run_id
# "local" / release_sha "unknown" and would be actively misleading if ever
# committed -- same reasoning as the SECURITY block above, not a new pattern.
data/quality/rx_pub_a0_reports_artifact_manifest.json

# Issue #274: these flow to Cloudflare R2 via sentinel-blogger.yml's
# r2_upload.py and generate-and-sync.yml's STAGE 9.5 (both already upload
# them every run, independent of any git commit) -- the Worker now reads
# them from R2 first (workers/intel-gateway/src/index.js's /api/ai/{filename}
# handler). Different reason from the block above (not a security/auth
# concern, just no longer serving any purpose): main's branch protection
# now requires PRs, so generate-and-sync.yml's direct `git push origin main`
# for these files was failing on every scheduled run, freezing whatever
# copy happened to be committed before that -- while the real (R2) serving
# path kept updating independently. Un-tracking removes a stale, misleading
# snapshot rather than fixing a push that had no reason to exist anymore.
api/ai/tracker.json
api/ai/health.json
api/ai/executive-brief.json
api/ai/monetization.json

# --- PIPELINE-CRITICAL STATE FILES — EXPLICITLY UN-IGNORED ---
# These MUST be committed so all downstream workflows find them on checkout.
!data/cache/

# P0 FIX: data/cache/feed_state.json moved OFF this un-ignore list, onto R2
# (scripts/r2_state_sync.py), same migration/reason as feed_manifest.json and
# processed_intel.json above -- "MUST be committed so downstream workflows
# find it on checkout" is no longer true: both workflows' checkouts now get
# it from R2 instead (downloaded before either workflow reads it), which
# main's branch ruleset can't reject the way it rejects a direct git push.
# This explicit ignore is required despite the file no longer being written
# here: without it, the broader `!data/cache/` un-ignore two lines up would
# still un-ignore it.
data/cache/feed_state.json

# P0 production-architecture-transformation mission (2026-09-08):
# data/cache/intel_index.json (intel_dedup_engine.py's dedup primary index,
# sibling to feed_state.json above, same atomic write contract) was
# previously deliberately kept on this un-ignore list ("explicitly out of
# scope for this migration" -- see the prior version of
# tests/test_r2_state_migration_wiring.py's test_unrelated_cache_file_is_
# unaffected) because git-based persistence was still assumed viable for
# it. That assumption is the same one that caused the 2026-08-26 staleness
# incident for feed_state.json/feed_manifest.json/processed_intel.json --
# main's branch ruleset rejects this file's direct push exactly the same
# way. Moved onto R2 (scripts/r2_state_sync.py's STATE_FILES) instead,
# same fix as feed_state.json immediately above. `git rm --cached` was
# also needed (see that command's own note) since the file was actually
# git-tracked, not just un-ignored in principle.
data/cache/intel_index.json

